;; A WORKED EXAMPLE shipped with the KIT — not a configuration. `bb init` copies the one
;; matching your seat (`--seat <name>`, default claude) to <name>-plan/profile.edn; edit it there.
;;
;; SEAT :claude. Its sibling is agy-ide.edn, and the pair is instructive: they
;; are near mirror images. Here Anthropic writes and Google and OpenAI verify,
;; every role over OpenRouter on the :openai shape; there Google writes and
;; Anthropic verifies, its Reviewer and spec reviewer direct to Anthropic on
;; the :anthropic shape. The pair still covers both tool-call shapes, which is
;; why there are two rather than one — and since this file's Anthropic roles
;; go through OpenRouter (below, and why), the :anthropic shape's worked
;; example is agy-ide.edn's reviewers, not anything here.
;;
;; Model names are dated selections (decision log P0-3: "Specific models are
;; dated selections"). The SHAPE is the part meant to outlive them. The
;; selections below were made by bake-offs during the KIT's development; their
;; records are not in this repository, so what each rests on is said here in
;; words and not in figures. `bb bake-off` is how to make your own.
;; The ROUTE of the Anthropic roles is a dated decision too: 2026-09-18, from a
;; paid probe (`NOTES.md` row 25), shipped here since 2026-09-25 (row 68).
;;
;; Three things this file is careful about:
;;
;;   ONE SEAT. The interactive client is where the human sits. The dispatched
;;   roles are reached over HTTP by the harness and are not anyone's editor —
;;   so there is exactly one :seat, and it is a scalar so it cannot grow a
;;   second. :seat names a tool `bb doctor` knows how to probe, which is why it
;;   is :claude rather than :claude-code — doctor probes binaries.
;;
;;   THE CODER'S FAMILY FOLLOWS THE SEAT, by convention and not by rule. You
;;   are already being helped by that family interactively, so letting it write
;;   the code too keeps one story; what must then differ is the VERIFIERS. It
;;   is not checked, because OpenCode 2 and Pi are bring-your-own-model clients
;;   with no inherent family — the relation is not expressible for half the
;;   seats this kit supports.
;;
;;   VERIFIER ≠ CODER FAMILY. Method §05 and decision log P0-3, and this one IS
;;   checked. The Tester and the Reviewer both verify the Coder, so neither may
;;   share its family, nor each other's. `bb profile` is the only place that
;;   rule has ever been anything but prose.
;;
;;   NO KEYS, EVER. :key-env NAMES an environment variable. This file is
;;   committed; a field that could hold a secret would eventually be handed one.
;;
;; WHY EVERY ROLE IS ON OPENROUTER, the Anthropic ones included. Four things
;; the money tooling can only do on that side:
;;
;;   ONE KEY AND ONE BALANCE. `bb balance`, and the two lines `run-loop run`
;;   prints around the loop, read OpenRouter's key limit and account credit
;;   from its API. Anthropic's API has no balance endpoint: a direct role's
;;   money is only ever SPEND, summed from the records after the fact.
;;
;;   THE COST IS REPORTED, NOT COMPUTED. OpenRouter's generation record
;;   carries what each completion cost, and the report prints it as the
;;   endpoint's word. Direct, the cost is usage × the profile's :pricing,
;;   marked ~ and only as fresh as the date the table was read. So NO
;;   :pricing on either Anthropic role here: `bb reprice` fills any step the
;;   record left unpriced from the generation records, and a price table
;;   would rot for nothing.
;;
;;   THE CREDIT STOP. A 402 stops the loop by name (:credit, owner human)
;;   with the endpoint and the key variable, not as one more failed dispatch.
;;   With every role on one key, one 402 stops everything — which is what a
;;   person wants — so check the balance against the plan's per-run floor
;;   (§13) before each run; the first adopter was stopped once on EACH
;;   provider's credit, mid-run, with nothing having warned.
;;
;;   THE GENERATION RECORD. A completion names no provider; OpenRouter's
;;   record says who served it, at which service tier, with native token
;;   counts and the host's own generation time — the provenance a direct
;;   endpoint cannot give. Its price is a fixed ~9 s wait at the end of every
;;   dispatch for the last completion's record (row 25).
;;
;; THE ROUNDS A DISPATCH MAY TAKE are this project's to set, per role, as
;; `:max-rounds n` in a role block below (none is set here: the harness's
;; default applies, 24). A round is one completion answering the last tool
;; result; the Tester, writing a test per target against a stub it cannot
;; run, takes more than the Coder. The first real project's Tester ended on
;; the default three times on a contract of fifty targets. The report's
;; "Dispatch rounds" line says how close each dispatch came.

{:seat :claude

 :roles
 {;; CODER — Claude Fable 5.1 at low effort, over OpenRouter pinned to
  ;; Anthropic's own serving. Effort dated 2026-09-14, the user's setting after
  ;; a day on both low and medium; the route dated 2026-09-18 (header).
  ;;
  ;; THE MODEL NAME IS OPENROUTER'S SLUG, `anthropic/claude-fable-5.1` with a
  ;; dot — not the API's `claude-fable-5-1`. The generation record resolves it
  ;; to the model that answered, and the report shows that one.
  ;;
  ;; PINNED TO "anthropic", NOT "google-vertex". Both serve this model. Pinned
  ;; to Vertex the prompt cache missed intermittently, each miss re-writing
  ;; the whole prompt at the cache-write premium; pinned
  ;; to Anthropic it never did, and cost no more than the direct route. With
  ;; :allow_fallbacks false the provider you logged yesterday is the one you
  ;; get today, and every report names the one that answered.
  ;;
  ;; CACHING IS ASKED FOR, HERE AND ON NO OTHER ROLE. On the :openai shape the
  ;; adapter merges :params into the request as they are and sends no
  ;; cache_control of its own — an Anthropic model behind this endpoint caches
  ;; only if the profile says so, and a profile that forgets turns caching
  ;; OFF silently, and the run costs more for it. The Coder's tool loop
  ;; re-sends the whole conversation every completion, so it is the role
  ;; the cache is for: most of its prompt tokens are cache reads, and a
  ;; record's token lines show the share (`bb report`).
  ;;
  ;; :max_tokens SITS IN :params on this shape (the adapter merges them; on
  ;; the :anthropic shape it is lifted out). Thinking counts against it, and
  ;; the adapter's default of 4096 would cut a turn off mid-thought — 16000,
  ;; the non-streaming ceiling the API guidance gives.
  :coder {:family :anthropic
          :model "anthropic/claude-fable-5.1"
          :shape :openai
          :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
          :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
          :params {:reasoning_effort "low"
                   :max_tokens 16000
                   :provider {:only ["anthropic"] :allow_fallbacks false}
                   :cache_control {:type "ephemeral"}}}

  ;; TESTER — gemini-3.8-flash at medium effort, over OpenRouter. Dated
  ;; selection 2026-09-16, when the Tester and Reviewer swapped families so the
  ;; best measured Reviewer could take that seat without sharing it.
  ;;
  ;; CHOOSE THE TESTER BY ITS TESTS, NOT ITS PRICE. The Tester writes MORE than
  ;; the Coder, from the contract alone, under more lint conventions; the
  ;; cheapest model in the hardest seat is how this kit lost a whole run. Medium
  ;; rather than low for the same reason: the cheaper tier's tests stopped
  ;; pinning the validate-once seam, which is a cent a dispatch for a Tester
  ;; that has stopped testing a rule.
  ;;
  ;; THE PROVIDER PIN IS THE POINT. Without :allow_fallbacks false the provider
  ;; you logged yesterday is not the one you get today, and a different provider
  ;; can mean a different chat template. One slug drew two different hosts
  ;; across three attempts of a single run, and "the model failed" was never a
  ;; safe reading after that.
  :tester {:family :google
           :model "google/gemini-3.8-flash"
           :shape :openai
           :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
           :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
           :params {:reasoning_effort "medium"
                    :provider {:only ["google-vertex"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}

  ;; REVIEWER — gpt-5.6-sol at high effort, over OpenRouter's standard endpoint.
  ;; Dated selection 2026-09-16.
  ;;
  ;; STANDARD, NOT FLEX. Flex was rate-limited upstream on most requests when
  ;; this was measured, and a verifier that fails for capacity rather than for
  ;; its findings is worth nothing. "openai" is also the provider's name, so the
  ;; pin is observed rather than guaranteed to exclude the flex and fast
  ;; endpoints; the report shows the service tier beside the provider, so a
  ;; routing change would be visible.
  ;;
  ;; HIGH, THOUGH MEDIUM MEASURED THE SAME on the defects that were tested. It
  ;; ships high by the user's decision, as insurance on defects unlike those:
  ;; the Reviewer runs once per unit of work and is the cheapest seat in the
  ;; loop.
  ;;
  ;; EFFORT IS A MEASURED SETTING, not a default — :reasoning_effort "high" on a
  ;; flash tier once produced minutes of thinking and an EMPTY final message
  ;; where the identical dispatch at medium answered quickly with real
  ;; findings. A flash tier spends a high budget thinking and has nothing left
  ;; to say with; this model showed no such shape at high.
  :reviewer {:family :openai
             :model "openai/gpt-5.6-sol"
             :shape :openai
             :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
             :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
             :params {:reasoning_effort "high"
                      :provider {:only ["openai"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}

  ;; SPEC REVIEWER — the same model as the Reviewer, over OpenRouter.
  ;; Dated selection 2026-09-18. ONE CALL PER SPEC, BEFORE START, no tools:
  ;; every place a target can be read two ways, every input the shapes allow
  ;; that no target mentions. `start` runs it for any spec without a current
  ;; review and stops for the Architect to read the list.
  ;;
  ;; NOT THE SEAT'S FAMILY, and `bb profile` checks it: the Architect who wrote
  ;; the spec sits in the seat, and a reviewer of its family reads a sentence
  ;; the way its author did. The spec's writer and its reviewer are different
  ;; families for the same reason the Tester is not the Coder's.
  ;;
  ;; WHY THIS MODEL, AND HIGH: measured. Tried on specs that had already been
  ;; dispatched it named every ambiguity that had cost a round; the
  ;; Architect's own family at low effort matched it on the one spec compared,
  ;; but is the seat's family. A cheaper choice here is possible and not yet
  ;; measured.
  :spec-reviewer {:family :openai
                  :model "openai/gpt-5.6-sol"
                  :shape :openai
                  :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
                  :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
                  :params {:reasoning_effort "high"
                           :provider {:only ["openai"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}

  ;; BLUEPRINT REVIEWER — Claude Opus 5.5 at high effort, over OpenRouter
  ;; pinned to Anthropic's own serving. Dated selection 2026-09-25. ONE CALL
  ;; PER BLUEPRINT, BEFORE SIGN-OFF, no tools: `bb blueprint-review` sends the
  ;; stage document and the Blueprint with the method's two questions — is it
  ;; over-engineered (§07 step 2), and do its shapes and targets follow §06's
  ;; rules — and writes the findings beside the stage's other reviews. The spec
  ;; review still reads every packet cut from it, one at a time, before dispatch;
  ;; this reads the whole, for what a packet cannot show.
  ;;
  ;; THE SEAT'S FAMILY, AND THAT IS ACCEPTED — the second such exception, with
  ;; the Orchestrator, and unlike that one §05's reasoning DOES reach it: a
  ;; Blueprint is the seat's, as a spec is, and the spec reviewer is held to
  ;; another family for exactly that. It was measured instead of assumed. On
  ;; one build three families read the plan and both Blueprints cold, and this
  ;; model — the seat's own family — was the one that found the load-bearing
  ;; defect of each Blueprint round alone, twice: the route library's conflict
  ;; semantics, which would have cost a run each; and it did so at more real
  ;; findings per dollar than the reader of record. What the rule feared, a
  ;; reader that reads a sentence the way its author did, did not show on
  ;; Blueprints. `bb profile` does not check this role's family; `NOTES.md`'s
  ;; register watches it, and what would reverse the decision is a Blueprint
  ;; defect this reader read past that another family caught.
  ;;
  ;; HIGH, CHOSEN: the reads that measured it ran at high; the model's default
  ;; is medium and the two have not been compared here. No :cache_control: one
  ;; completion, read once. No :pricing: the endpoint reports the cost.
  :blueprint-reviewer {:family :anthropic
                       :model "anthropic/claude-opus-5.5"
                       :shape :openai
                       :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
                       :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
                       :params {:reasoning_effort "high"
                                :max_tokens 16000
                                :provider {:only ["anthropic"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}

  ;; ORCHESTRATOR — Claude Fable 5.1 at low effort, over OpenRouter pinned to
  ;; Anthropic's own serving, as the Coder is and for the header's reasons.
  ;; Dated selection 2026-09-16; the route 2026-09-18. TRIAGE ONLY: one
  ;; completion with no tools, on a red gate or a note, answering who owns it —
  ;; coder, tester, architect or human — with a reason and guidance for the
  ;; retried role. Dispatch itself is software (harness.loop.orchestrate); this
  ;; is the one judgement inside the loop that a mechanical proposal cannot make.
  ;;
  ;; IT SHARES THE CODER'S FAMILY, AND THAT IS ACCEPTED. §05's rule is for
  ;; verifiers: the Tester and the Reviewer exist to disagree with the Coder.
  ;; Triage neither writes code nor judges a diff; it reads a failure against
  ;; the contract and both roles' files. The decision (2026-09-16) is that
  ;; the family that reads a Clojure contract best should route, and the
  ;; safety is that an `architect` or `human` route stops the loop for a
  ;; person — a wrong route costs one paid attempt, not a merge. What is
  ;; watched: a triage that sides with the Coder's reading of the contract
  ;; over the Tester's where the Tester was right. `NOTES.md`'s register has
  ;; the row.
  ;;
  ;; NO :cache_control HERE, ON PURPOSE. A cache write costs a premium over
  ;; the input rate, and triage is one completion with no tools: the prompt
  ;; is read once and never again, so asking for the cache buys nothing and
  ;; pays the premium: cached triage cost more than uncached on the prompts
  ;; replayed. The same block as the Coder's otherwise, one pin for both
  ;; roles; Vertex would do here (caching is irrelevant to it, and the routes
  ;; it gave were the same) if latency ever matters.
  ;;
  ;; LOW, MEASURED — high until 2026-09-18, "because this is judgement work".
  ;; The judgement did not differ: the first adopter's triage prompts, re-asked
  ;; at low, blind, gave the same route on all but one uncontested call, the
  ;; same split on the contested one, and on the one disagreement the better
  ;; route under the rule that an ambiguous contract is the Architect's. The
  ;; saving is small: triage's cost is its prompt, and effort changes only what
  ;; the model writes, which here is one JSON block. :max_tokens as the Coder's.
  :orchestrator {:family :anthropic
                 :model "anthropic/claude-fable-5.1"
                 :shape :openai
                 :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
                 :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
                 :params {:reasoning_effort "low"
                          :max_tokens 16000
                          :provider {:only ["anthropic"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}}}
